`EngineUpdateOptionsSchema.where` is typed as an object/FilterCondition, but the engine still treats `options.where` as possibly being a string ID (`typeof options.where === 'string'`). This branch is effectively unreachable for schema-validated callers and makes the ID-extraction semantics ambiguous. Consider either (a) removing string-ID support and requiring `id` or `where: { id: ... }`, or (b) explicitly allowing `where` to be a string in the schema/type if you want to support the shorthand.
```suggestion
     if (!id && options?.where && (options.where as any).id) {
         id = (options.where as any).id;
```

`EngineDeleteOptionsSchema.where` is typed as an object/FilterCondition, but the engine still treats `options.where` as possibly being a string ID (`typeof options.where === 'string'`). For schema-validated callers this is unreachable and blurs whether delete-by-id should use the `id` argument vs `where`. Consider removing the string branch or updating the schema/type to explicitly support it.
```suggestion
    if (options?.where && (options.where as any).id) {
      id = (options.where as any).id;
    }
```

`find()` now forwards `{ object, ...query }` directly to the driver. This drops the previous `top → limit` alias handling, but drivers (e.g. SqlDriver) explicitly ignore `top` and only honor `limit`. As a result, requests using OData-style `top` will return unbounded results. Consider normalizing in the engine (e.g., if `query.top` is set and `query.limit` is not, set `ast.limit = query.top` and delete `ast.top`) before calling the driver.
```suggestion

    // Normalize OData-style `top` to `limit` for drivers that only honor `limit`
    const anyAst = ast as any;
    if (anyAst.top != null && anyAst.limit == null) {
      anyAst.limit = anyAst.top;
      delete anyAst.top;
    }
```

The legacy `populate`/`expand` normalization can drop expansions when both `populate` and a string `expand` are provided: `expandNames` is collected from `populate`, but because `options.expand` is a truthy string, the code skips creating the `expand` record and then deletes `options.expand` as a non-object, leaving no `expand` at all. Since `populate` is intended to take precedence, consider treating non-object `options.expand` as absent (or deleting it before the `if (expandNames.length > 0 && !options.expand)` check) so `populate` expansions are preserved.
```suggestion
        // Treat non-object `expand` as absent so legacy populate/expand values
        // can be normalized into a proper expand record. This preserves any
        // explicitly provided object-shaped expand (advanced usage).
        if (typeof options.expand !== 'object' || options.expand === null) {
            delete options.expand;
        }
        // Only set expand if not already an object (advanced usage)
        if (expandNames.length > 0 && !options.expand) {
            options.expand = {} as Record<string, any>;
            for (const rel of expandNames) {
                options.expand[rel] = { object: rel };
            }
```
